+# Hegel PBT Report: indexmap
+
+Target: `indexmap` 2.14.0 (insertion-order-preserving hash map/set), local checkout.
+Tests added to `tests/quick.rs` (module `hegel_props`) alongside the existing quickcheck suite.
+Dependency: `hegeltest` 0.28.2 added with `--ignore-rust-version` (hegeltest MSRV 1.86 vs the crate's `rust-version = "1.85"` — dev-dependency only; worth flagging to maintainers if ever upstreamed).
+
+Consent check: no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, `.ai-ack`, or anti-AI clause in README/CONTRIBUTING/LICENSE — proceeded.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+The existing quickcheck suite is strong (39 properties, including per-op order checks for `swap_indices`/`move_index` and a HashMap-oracle ops test). The main gap: the ops-sequence tests (`operations_i8` etc.) use only a std `HashMap` oracle, so they verify the key→value mapping but **not iteration order** across interleaved operations. The hegel tests center on closing that gap with stateful model tests whose oracle carries order.
+
+1. **`map_model` (stateful, the core test).** `IndexMap<u8, i32>` vs a dual oracle: `Vec<(u8, i32)>` (insertion order) + `std::HashMap` (mapping). 13 rules: `insert` (existing key keeps position, value updated, old value returned — checked against `Vec` position + `mem::replace`), `swap_remove` (oracle: `Vec::swap_remove` — last entry into the hole), `shift_remove` (oracle: `Vec::remove` — tail shifts down), `get`, `get_index` (drawn up to len+2 to also check `None`; index→key→index round-trip), `swap_indices` (oracle `Vec::swap`), `move_index` (oracle `Vec::remove`+`insert`), `sort_keys`/`sort_unstable_keys` (drawn choice; unique keys ⇒ both must equal the model sort), `reverse`, `truncate` (drawn past len to check the no-op case), `pop`, `retain` (drawn threshold predicate). Invariant after every rule: equal `len` across all three, **exact iteration-order equality with the Vec**, and per-entry `get_index(i)`/`get_index_of(k)`/`get(k)` round-trips plus HashMap agreement. Keys drawn with ~50% bias toward present keys so update/remove paths regularly hit.
+2. **`set_model` (stateful).** `IndexSet<u8>` mirror against `Vec<u8>` + `HashSet<u8>`: insert (returns `bool`), swap_remove/shift_remove (return `bool`), move_index, swap_indices, sort/sort_unstable, reverse, truncate, pop, retain; same per-step order + round-trip invariant.
+3. **`insert_only_iteration_matches_insertion_order`.** Insert-only sequences with i64 keys (biased toward a small subdomain so duplicate keys occur): iteration order == first-occurrence order, each key holds its last value. Broadens the quickcheck `insertion_order` (u32 keys, unit values, no value check).
+4. **`swap_remove_vs_shift_remove` (differential).** Same map, same key (present ~50%): both return the same value and leave the same key-value *set*; each result exactly matches its `Vec` counterpart (`Vec::swap_remove` / `Vec::remove`); absent key leaves both maps untouched.
+5. **`insert_existing_key_keeps_position`.** Re-inserting key at index i: returns old value, len unchanged, `get_index_of == Some(i)`, whole key order unchanged, new value visible.
+6. **`split_off_partitions_in_order`.** Head keeps `[0, at)`, tail gets `[at, len)`, both in original order, hash lookups (`get_index_of`) work in both halves, no cross-contamination.
+7. **`binary_search_keys_agrees_with_get_index_of`.** After `sort_unstable_keys`: `Ok(i)` agrees with `get_index_of`; `Err(i)` implies absent and is a correct insertion point (all before < probe < all after).
+8. **Set-op iteration order (4 tests).** `union` (self order, then other-unique in other order), `intersection` (self order), `difference` (self order), `symmetric_difference` (self-only in self order, then other-only in other order) — each against the documented order oracle plus a `HashSet` set-equality oracle. Elements biased toward a small subdomain so overlaps actually occur. The quickcheck suite does not cover set-op order at all.
+
+## Test results
+
+All 11 hegel tests pass, and the full pre-existing suite (134 unit + 39 quickcheck + others) stays green.
+
+- **Liveness:** all 11 tests verified live by mutation — one deliberate oracle/assertion mutation per test (e.g. map machine's swap_remove oracle changed to `Vec::remove`; set machine's move_index oracle changed to `swap`; set-op filters inverted; guarded branches mutated *inside* the guard). All 11 mutations produced failures with shrunk counterexamples — e.g. the map-machine mutation shrank to a minimal 4-step sequence (insert 0, insert 1, insert 2, swap_remove 0). One subtlety: on one random-seed batch run the two machine mutations happened not to fail (seed luck); repeated runs failed 3/3, and the committed tests keep hegel's default seeding.
+- **Vacuity:** mutation testing was designed to fire inside guards (present-key branch, `Ok`/`Err` branches, overlap-dependent set-op branches) — all fired. It also exposed one genuinely vacuous generator during review: full-range i64 keys never produce duplicate keys, so the update-in-place branch of test 3 never ran; fixed by biasing keys through a `one_of!` with a small subdomain (same fix already present in `two_sets`).
+- **Exploratory 10x run:** all 11 tests at `test_cases = 1000` with per-test atomic case counters printed and verified (1000–1037 cases each). All green in 1.6 s. Reverted to the default 100.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Every property held at the default and 10x case counts. Consistent with expectations for this crate: it is heavily tested (39 quickcheck properties, fuzzing via `arbitrary`, wide production use), and the ordering semantics under test are exactly the crate's headline feature.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned as unsound. One property was *repaired* rather than abandoned: the insert-only order test's original generator made its duplicate-key clause unfalsifiable in practice (vacuous branch, not an unsound assertion) — fixed via key-domain biasing.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The stateful `#[hegel::state_machine]` API mapped cleanly onto the Vec+HashMap dual-oracle pattern; the plain `&TestCase` helper (`draw_key` with present-key bias) from the Rust reference was exactly what was needed for hit-rate control in rules.
+- The reference's MSRV note (`--ignore-rust-version`) applied verbatim (1.85 crate vs 1.86 hegeltest).
+- Worth emphasizing in SKILL.md: a green *batch* mutation run can under-report — one seed-lucky pass made two live machines look dead until re-run. The existing advice to confirm a shrunk counterexample (not just red/green) covers this, but "re-run a surprising mutation survivor before investigating" would have saved a diagnosis cycle.
+- Verbose mode's `Step N: <rule>` output made machine behavior instantly auditable; good that the reference documents `verbosity = hegel::Verbosity::Verbose` needs the qualified path.
